---
/**
 * /tools/ppn-002-calculator
 *
 * The only page on the site that produces a NUMBER a user will quote to a
 * buyer. The arithmetic lives in lib/ppn002.ts, ported line for line from
 * js/tool-engine-ppn-002-calculator.js and gated by a test that drives the
 * legacy page and this one with identical inputs and compares what each
 * renders. A content page that drifts reads differently; this one would tell
 * somebody their evaluation clears a statutory floor when it does not.
 *
 * WHAT THE PORT CHANGES, AND WHY EACH IS SAFE:
 *
 * 1. THE RESULT IS MARKUP, NOT AN innerHTML STRING. The legacy engine builds
 *    ~25 lines of HTML with inline styles, then walks the result re-applying
 *    every declaration with !important to defeat a light-section stylesheet.
 *    That whole mechanism exists because the old CSS had no layer order. Here
 *    the result is real elements in the cascade, so none of it is needed.
 *
 * 2. THE ERROR IS role="alert", THE RESULT role="status". Already correct in
 *    the legacy engine and preserved: an error interrupts, a result does not.
 *
 * 3. NO SOFT-WALL GATE. The legacy engine calls CAToolTeaser.gateSoftWall
 *    before calculating. This page states "no account, no email gate" four
 *    times over, so a gate that can interrupt the calculation contradicts the
 *    page. If a gate is wanted back, the copy has to change with it.
 *
 * IT MUST NEVER ASSERT COMPLIANCE. Whether a procurement is compliant is the
 * contracting authority's judgement. The tool reports whether a number clears
 * the floor and stops. See the note in lib/ppn002.ts.
 *
 * WHAT THE PORT LOST, RESTORED 2026-08-04 after the owner reported the tool as
 * "not working" with "drop down visibility" problems. Three separate things, and
 * only one of them was on this page:
 *
 *   1. THE RESULT RENDERED OFF-SCREEN. Measured at 1440x900: the Calculate
 *      button sits at y=771, so it is clicked without scrolling, and the card
 *      then lands at y=889.5 with 2% of it above the fold. The legacy engine
 *      scrolled the card into view; the port dropped that line. Pressing the
 *      button changed nothing you could see. See `reveal()` below.
 *
 *   2. ONE ERROR SENTENCE FOR EVERY KIND OF MISTAKE, and no mark on the field
 *      that caused it. Now per-field, from `problems()` in lib/ppn002.ts, with
 *      aria-invalid set and focus moved. The validation RULE did not change.
 *
 *   3. THE MISSION DROPDOWN OPENED WHITE-ON-WHITE. That was never this page's
 *      bug — it belonged to `.field__input`, which all three selects on the site
 *      share — so it is fixed in styles/forms.css, where the measurement and the
 *      Chromium behaviour behind it are written up in full.
 */
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../../../components/ui/Button.astro';
import FinalCta from '../../../components/sections/FinalCta.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../../../lib/schema';
import { SITE } from '../../../data/site';
import { MISSIONS } from '../../../lib/ppn002';
import '../../../styles/forms.css';

const title = 'PPN 002 Social Value Calculator | Free, no account | CrowAgent';
const description =
  'Free PPN 002 social value calculator. Check a tender evaluation against the mandatory 10% minimum social-value weighting. No account, no email gate.';

/*
 * THE TRACEABILITY LEDGER — restored from tools/ppn-002-calculator/index.html:
 * 180-192 (OA-28, 2026-08-02). The port dropped it, and the allow-list recorded
 * it as "folded into the calculator intro copy", which it was not: the intro
 * says the tool is free and what it reports, and says nothing about where any
 * figure comes from. This block IS the page's provenance claim, and dropping it
 * from the one page on the site that emits a number a bidder will quote is the
 * wrong block to lose.
 *
 * ONE WORDING CHANGE, AND ONLY ONE. The live row 2 ends "the framework used by
 * major UK contracting authorities to evaluate social value" — an unqualified
 * framework claim of the kind OA-15 covers. The hedge already published on
 * /crowmark and already emitted by this page's own result card ("aligned to
 * National TOMs conventions; not a full National TOMs implementation") is
 * appended, so one engine is described one way everywhere. No new claim is made;
 * a claim is narrowed. Everything else is verbatim.
 *
 * The live block also closes with an "indicative estimate, not legal or
 * procurement advice" line. That sentence already ships in `.basis` above, so it
 * is not repeated here.
 */
const LEDGER = [
  {
    ref: 'PPN 002',
    lead: 'Minimum 10% social-value weighting',
    body: 'of the total tender evaluation score for in-scope central government contracts, mandatory from 1 October 2025. The floor is always 10%, applied to your total evaluation weighting.',
    source: 'Cabinet Office',
    detail: '10% minimum',
  },
  {
    ref: 'National TOMs',
    lead: 'Themes, Outcomes and Measures',
    body: 'aligns your chosen mission to the framework major UK contracting authorities use to evaluate social value. Measures here are aligned to National TOMs conventions; this is not a full National TOMs implementation.',
    source: 'Social Value Portal',
    detail: 'TOMs framework',
  },
  {
    ref: 'National TOMs',
    lead: 'National TOMs proxy values',
    body: 'underpin the monetary weightings CrowMark uses in the full product. This free scorer gives the indicative floor check.',
    source: 'National TOMs 2023-24',
    detail: 'Proxy values',
  },
];

const schema = [
  /*
   * WebApplication. Published by the legacy page and lost in the port
   * (OA-24). isAccessibleForFree and the zero-price Offer are the fields that
   * make "free, no account" machine-readable, which is the page's whole claim.
   */
  {
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'PPN 002 Social Value Scorer',
    applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Web',
    url: `${SITE.origin}/tools/ppn-002-calculator`,
    description,
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'GBP' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CrowAgent', url: `${SITE.origin}/` },
  },
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Tools', path: '/tools' },
    { name: 'PPN 002 Calculator', path: '/tools/ppn-002-calculator' },
  ]),
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/tools/ppn-002-calculator" schema={schema}>
  <Section
    as="h1"
    eyebrow="Free, no account"
    title="PPN 002 Social Value Scorer"
    standfirst="Enter the total evaluation weighting and the social-value weighting within it. The tool reports where that sits against the mandatory 10% minimum PPN 002 sets. No sign-up, no email gate."
    id="calculator"
  >
    <form class="form calc" id="ppn-form" novalidate>
      <div class="field">
        <label class="field__label" for="missionType">
          Bid mission
          <span class="field__req" aria-hidden="true">*</span>
          <span class="visually-hidden">(required)</span>
        </label>
        <select class="field__input" id="missionType" name="missionType" required aria-describedby="calc-err">
          {MISSIONS.map((m) => <option value={m.value}>{m.label}</option>)}
        </select>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label class="field__label" for="bidWeighting">
          Total bid weighting (%)
          <span class="field__req" aria-hidden="true">*</span>
          <span class="visually-hidden">(required)</span>
        </label>
        <input
          class="field__input"
          id="bidWeighting"
          name="bidWeighting"
          type="number"
          inputmode="numeric"
          min="0"
          max="100"
          step="1"
          placeholder="e.g. 100"
          required
          aria-describedby="bidWeighting-help calc-err"
        />
        {/* RESTORED FROM THE REFERENCE BUILD, tools/ppn-002-calculator/index.html:153.
            The port replaced "Usually 100% for the total tender evaluation." with "The
            share of the total evaluation score covered by the whole tender, as a
            percentage.", which defines the field in terms of itself and never tells
            anyone what to type. The reference wording names the number a user almost
            always wants, which is the difference between a form you can fill in and one
            you abandon. */}
        <p class="field__help" id="bidWeighting-help">
          Usually 100% for the total tender evaluation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label class="field__label" for="socialValueCommitment">
          Social-value weighting in tender (%)
          <span class="field__req" aria-hidden="true">*</span>
          <span class="visually-hidden">(required)</span>
        </label>
        <input
          class="field__input"
          id="socialValueCommitment"
          name="socialValueCommitment"
          type="number"
          inputmode="numeric"
          min="0"
          max="100"
          step="1"
          placeholder="e.g. 10"
          required
          aria-describedby="sv-help calc-err"
        />
        {/* The reference build's sentence (index.html:159) said what the field IS; the
            port's said only what it may not exceed. Both facts matter, so both are here,
            in that order. The constraint is also raised against this field by name when
            it is broken, rather than only sitting in the hint. */}
        <p class="field__help" id="sv-help">
          The percentage of the total score allocated to social value. It cannot be larger
          than the total bid weighting.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <Button type="submit">Calculate</Button>
        <Button type="reset" variant="secondary">Reset</Button>
      </div>

      {/* An error interrupts; a result does not. Two regions, two roles. */}
      <p class="field__err calc__err" id="calc-err" role="alert" hidden></p>

      {/* This form has no action and computes entirely in the browser, so with
          scripts off pressing Calculate reloads the page with the answers wiped
          and nothing to show for it. Every other form on the site already says
          so through .form__noscript; this one failed silently. It carries no
          Cloudflare check because nothing is submitted anywhere: the numbers
          never leave the page, which is why the tool needs no sign-up. */}
      <noscript>
        <p class="form__noscript">
          This calculator works out the answer in your browser, so it needs JavaScript. The rule it
          applies is simple enough to check by hand: PPN 002 sets a minimum social-value weighting
          of 10% of the total tender evaluation score.
        </p>
      </noscript>
    </form>

    <div id="tool-result" class="result surface surface--panel" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>

    <p class="basis">
      Basis: Procurement Policy Note PPN 002 (published 13 February 2025), which mandates a minimum 10%
      social-value weighting of the total tender evaluation score for in-scope contracts, and is
      mandatory from 1 October 2025. Indicative estimate: not legal or procurement advice.
      Verify against your contracting authority's evaluation model.
    </p>

    <p class="more">
      <a href="/tools/ppn-002-calculator/methodology/">Read the full methodology</a>
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="How it is scored"
    title="Every figure traces back to its source."
    standfirst="The scorer applies the PPN 002 rule to your inputs. Nothing is invented; each number cites the framework behind it."
    id="methodology"
  >
    <dl class="ledger">
      {
        LEDGER.map((r) => (
          <div class="ledger__row">
            <dt class="meta ledger__ref">{r.ref}</dt>
            <dd class="ledger__desc">
              <strong>{r.lead}</strong> {r.body}
            </dd>
            <dd class="ledger__meta">
              {r.source}
              <span class="ledger__detail">{r.detail}</span>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </Section>

  {/*
    THE CLOSING CTA, WITH ITS OWN HEADING BACK (OA-28, 2026-08-02). The port kept
    the two buttons but retitled the block "The same engine, across the whole
    bid.", so the question the live page closes on — "Need the full scoring
    engine?" — and the two onward links under it went. Heading, eyebrow, copy and
    both links are restored from tools/ppn-002-calculator/index.html:176-192.
  */}
  <FinalCta
    eyebrow="Go further"
    title="Need the full scoring engine?"
    standfirst="CrowMark drafts social value narratives cited to their sources, values commitments against National TOMs proxy values, and models scenarios against the 10% floor."
    id="product"
    links={[
      { label: 'All free tools', href: '/tools' },
      { label: 'How the score is calculated', href: '/tools/ppn-002-calculator/methodology/' },
    ]}
  >
    <Button href="/contact?enquiry=limited-access#contact-form">Request access</Button>
    <Button href="/crowmark" variant="secondary">Explore CrowMark</Button>
  </FinalCta>
</Base>

<script>
  import { calculate, problems, verdict, pct, pts, FLOOR_PCT, MISSIONS } from '../../../lib/ppn002';
  import type { Ppn002Field } from '../../../lib/ppn002';

  /** value -> label, derived from the same list that renders the <select>. */
  const MISSION_LABELS: Record<string, string> = Object.fromEntries(
    MISSIONS.filter((m) => m.value).map((m) => [m.value, m.label])
  );

  /** DOM order, which is the order errors are read in and focus is moved in. */
  const FIELDS: Ppn002Field[] = ['missionType', 'bidWeighting', 'socialValueCommitment'];

  /*
   * Read once, the same way scripts/motion.ts and scripts/magnetic.ts read it. A
   * user who changes the setting mid-visit gets the new behaviour on reload; a
   * user who has it set never sees this page move under them.
   */
  const REDUCED =
    typeof window.matchMedia === 'function' &&
    window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  function initCalculator() {
    const form = document.getElementById('ppn-form') as HTMLFormElement | null;
    const out = document.getElementById('tool-result');
    const err = document.getElementById('calc-err');
    if (!form || !out || !err) return;

    const field = (id: Ppn002Field) =>
      document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement | HTMLSelectElement | null;

    const val = (id: Ppn002Field) => field(id)?.value ?? '';

    /*
     * THE RESULT USED TO RENDER WHERE NOBODY COULD SEE IT. Measured on the built
     * page at 1440x900: the Calculate button sits at y=771, so it needs no
     * scrolling to click, and the result card then lands at y=889.5 in a 900px
     * viewport — 2% of it on screen. The page did not move, so pressing Calculate
     * looked like pressing a dead button. The legacy engine scrolled the card into
     * view (js/tool-engine-ppn-002-calculator.js:148) and the port dropped it.
     *
     * IT SCROLLS ONLY WHEN IT HAS TO. Running the tool a second time with the card
     * already on screen must not yank the page; the geometry is measured first and
     * a card that is already fully visible is left alone.
     *
     * THE THRESHOLD IS THE HEADER, NOT THE SCROLL PADDING, and the two are not the
     * same number. <html> carries scroll-padding-top: 96px (Base.astro), but the
     * card MEASURES at top: 76 once a scroll has settled, because the section's
     * arrival transform is still resolving when the scroll is issued. The sticky
     * header is 73px, so 76 clears it and the card is genuinely visible; testing
     * against 96 would call that hidden and re-scroll to the same place on every
     * submit. Visible means "not under the header".
     *
     * TWO FRAMES, NOT ONE. The element is unhidden immediately before this runs,
     * and its box is not final until the frame after the one that lays it out.
     * Measuring in the first frame reads the pre-layout rect.
     */
    const HEADER_HEIGHT = 73;
    const reveal = (el: HTMLElement) => {
      requestAnimationFrame(() =>
        requestAnimationFrame(() => {
          const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();
          if (r.top >= HEADER_HEIGHT && r.bottom <= window.innerHeight) return;
          el.scrollIntoView({ behavior: REDUCED ? 'auto' : 'smooth', block: 'start' });
        })
      );
    };

    /* Emptied, not just hidden. `role="alert"` announces a CHANGE, so leaving the
       previous sentence in the node means the same mistake made twice in a row is
       silent the second time: hidden -> visible is not a text mutation. */
    const clearError = () => {
      err.hidden = true;
      err.textContent = '';
      FIELDS.forEach((id) => field(id)?.removeAttribute('aria-invalid'));
    };

    const clearResult = () => {
      out.hidden = true;
      out.replaceChildren();
    };

    form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();

      const mission = val('missionType');
      const total = parseFloat(val('bidWeighting'));
      const sv = parseFloat(val('socialValueCommitment'));

      clearError();

      /*
       * The offending fields are NAMED, marked and focused. `.field__input
       * [aria-invalid='true']` has existed in styles/forms.css since the contact
       * form shipped and this page never set the attribute, so the shared error
       * affordance was carrying no weight here. Colour is still the second signal:
       * the message text is the first, and it now says which field it is about.
       */
      const bad = problems(mission, total, sv);
      if (bad.length > 0) {
        clearResult();
        bad.forEach((p) => field(p.field)?.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true'));
        err.hidden = false;
        err.textContent = bad.map((p) => p.message).join(' ');
        /* WCAG 3.3.1: put the user at the first thing they have to change. */
        field(bad[0].field)?.focus();
        return;
      }

      const r = calculate(mission, total, sv);
      /* Unreachable while `calculate()` refuses exactly what `problems()` reports,
         which it does by calling it. Stated rather than assumed, because the one
         thing this page must never do is render a card built from a null result. */
      if (!r) {
        clearResult();
        err.hidden = false;
        err.textContent =
          'Those figures could not be scored. Check the weightings and try again.';
        return;
      }

      // Built as elements rather than an innerHTML string. The legacy engine
      // assembled ~25 lines of inline-styled HTML and then re-applied every
      // declaration with !important to beat a light-section stylesheet; none of
      // that is needed once the CSS has a layer order.
      //
      // ASSEMBLED IN A FRAGMENT AND INSERTED ONCE. `#tool-result` is
      // `aria-live="polite"`, and appending eight nodes one at a time queues eight
      // announcements of the pieces of one answer. One insertion is one answer.
      const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();

      const add = (cls: string, text: string, tag = 'p') => {
        const el = document.createElement(tag);
        el.className = cls;
        el.textContent = text;
        frag.appendChild(el);
        return el;
      };

      /*
       * STRUCTURE MATCHES THE LEGACY CARD FIELD FOR FIELD. A first pass showed
       * the share-of-total in the second cell where the legacy shows the raw
       * weighting in points, and omitted the "Scored mission" block entirely.
       * Both were caught by driving the two calculators side by side rather
       * than by reading the code.
       */
      add('meta result__kicker', 'Your proposed social-value weighting');
      add('result__headline', pct(sv));
      add(
        'result__sub',
        `Against the PPN 002 mandatory ${FLOOR_PCT}% floor, which equals ${pct(r.floorPoints)} of the total score on a ${pts(total)}-point evaluation.`
      );

      const grid = document.createElement('dl');
      grid.className = 'result__grid';
      /* Placed in the fragment now and filled below. Order in the card is
         decided here, once, rather than by when each cell happens to be built. */
      frag.appendChild(grid);
      const cell = (term: string, value: string, note: string) => {
        const wrap = document.createElement('div');
        const dt = document.createElement('dt');
        /* `meta`, the third of the three shared label recipes in
           styles/labels.css. This cell is built by script, so the class has to
           be set here; before 2026-08-04 the stylesheet restated the recipe for
           it instead, at 0.08em against the shared 0.1em. */
        dt.className = 'meta';
        dt.textContent = term;
        const dd = document.createElement('dd');
        dd.textContent = value;
        /* The note goes INSIDE the <dd>. A <div> in a <dl> may contain only
           <dt> and <dd>; a third sibling is invalid and axe reports
           `definition-list`. Caught by running axe against the RESULT state
           rather than only the empty page — the grid does not exist until a
           calculation has run. */
        const small = document.createElement('span');
        small.className = 'result__note';
        small.textContent = note;
        dd.appendChild(small);
        wrap.append(dt, dd);
        grid.appendChild(wrap);
      };
      cell(
        `Mandatory ${FLOOR_PCT}% floor`,
        `${pts(r.floorPoints)} pts`,
        `${FLOOR_PCT}% of ${pts(total)} total points`
      );
      cell(
        'Your proposed weighting',
        `${pts(sv)} pts`,
        r.clearsFloor ? 'At or above the floor' : `${pts(r.shortfall)} pts below the floor`
      );

      /* The state is carried by TEXT as well as colour. "At or above" / "Below"
         is the whole finding and must not depend on seeing green or amber. */
      const tag = add(
        `chip result__tag result__tag--${r.clearsFloor ? 'ok' : 'under'}`,
        r.clearsFloor ? `At or above the ${FLOOR_PCT}% floor` : `Below the ${FLOOR_PCT}% floor`
      );
      tag.after(
        Object.assign(document.createElement('p'), {
          className: 'result__verdict',
          textContent: verdict(sv, r),
        })
      );

      /*
       * THE MISSION BLOCK, WITH THE FRAMEWORK CLAIM HEDGED. The legacy card
       * ends this line "mapped to the Social Value TOMs framework" — an
       * unqualified framework claim on a free public tool, and the same pattern
       * as OA-15. /crowmark is careful about exactly this: "aligned to National
       * TOMs conventions" and "not a full National TOMs implementation". That
       * already-published wording is used here so one engine is described one
       * way everywhere. No new claim is made; a claim is narrowed.
       */
      add('meta result__kicker result__kicker--tight', 'Scored mission');
      add(
        'result__mission',
        `${MISSION_LABELS[mission] ?? mission}. Measures are aligned to National TOMs conventions; this is not a full National TOMs implementation.`
      );

      /* Unhidden while still empty, then filled in one operation: a live region
         has to be in the accessibility tree BEFORE the content lands in it, or
         the change it is meant to announce happened while it was not there. */
      out.hidden = false;
      out.replaceChildren(frag);
      reveal(out);
    });

    form.addEventListener('reset', () => {
      /* The browser clears the fields; this clears everything the browser does
         not know about — the card, the message, and the invalid marks on the
         controls. A reset that leaves a field still flagged red is worse than no
         reset at all. */
      clearResult();
      clearError();
    });
  }

  initCalculator();
</script>

<style>

  /* Centred to match the head block above it (owner decision, 2026-08-02), and
     to match the live block this restores. Alignment only. */

  /* ── CENTRED, LIKE EVERYTHING ABOVE AND BELOW IT ──────────────────────────
   *
   * A 560px form in a full-measure container hugs the left edge. Measured at
   * 1440 it sat at 188..748 with 560px of empty page beside it, between a
   * centred head above and a centred section below — the most visible instance
   * of this on the site, and the third found in three routes after /compare and
   * /faq.
   *
   * The fields, labels and help text stay left-aligned, because a form is read
   * and filled in down a left edge. What moves is the BLOCK. */
  .calc { max-width: 560px; margin-inline: auto; }

  /* ── Traceability ledger ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Three columns: the reference, what it says, and where it comes from. A <dl>
     because each row is one reference and two facts about it, and the pairing is
     the whole point of a provenance table. */
  .ledger { margin: 0; display: grid; }

  .ledger__row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.6fr) minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 0.7fr);
    gap: var(--stack-1) clamp(16px, 2.5vw, 32px);
    padding: var(--stack-2) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .ledger__row:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--c-border); }

  .ledger__ref {
    color: var(--c-ambient-start);
  }

  .ledger__desc {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .ledger__desc strong { color: var(--c-text); font-weight: 600; }

  .ledger__meta {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .ledger__detail { display: block; margin-top: 4px; }

  /* ── Onward links under the closing CTA ───────────────────────────────── */

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .ledger__row { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }


  .calc__err[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* An opaque panel, not glass: the result has to stay readable over whatever
     is behind it. Treatment from `surface surface--panel`. */
  .result {
    /* Centred, not left-pinned. `margin-top` alone left this 144px off axis
       under a head block that was centred at 0. */
    margin: var(--sec-gap) auto 0;
    padding: var(--blk-pad);
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
  }
  .result[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* THE RECIPE IS GONE FROM HERE, 2026-08-04, A-48. It tracked at 0.14em, the
     dt below it at 0.08em and the tag under that at 0.1em — three tracking
     values inside ONE result card, for three labels a reader takes as one
     family. The three elements carry `meta`, `meta` and `chip` now.
     NONE OF IT WAS VISIBLE TO THE RENDERED RULE. This panel is `hidden` until a
     calculation runs, and rule 2 of check-treatments.js skips anything not
     displayed — rightly, because it measures what a reader sees. The fork was
     real and the gate was structurally unable to reach it. Rule 8 reads the
     source, which is the only instrument that can. */
  .result :global(.result__kicker) {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    color: var(--c-ambient-start);
  }

  .result :global(.result__headline) {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-stat);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--c-text);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .result :global(.result__sub) {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 var(--stack-2);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .result :global(.result__grid) {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-2);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--stack-2);
  }
  /* Recipe gone, 2026-08-04, A-48: the <dt> carries `meta`. Colour stays,
     because colour is deliberately not part of any of the three recipes. */
  .result :global(.result__grid dt) { color: var(--c-text-muted); }
  .result :global(.result__grid dd) {
    margin: 4px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .result :global(.result__note) {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 4px;
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* THE PILL IS `.chip` NOW, 2026-08-04, A-48. This was one of the eleven pill
     geometries labels.css was written to end - 4px/11px against the shared
     4.8px/11.2px, weight 600 against 500, tracking 0.1em against 0.08em - and
     it survived the consolidation only because the panel is hidden until a
     calculation runs, so the rendered rule never saw it.
     THE BORDER COLOUR STAYS AND IT IS NOT A LEFTOVER. `.chip` draws its rule in
     --c-border on purpose: a chip's hue is its MEANING, so the shared recipe
     states none and the call site decides. Here the verdict is the meaning -
     teal at or above the floor, amber below it - and `currentColor` carries the
     same decision to the outline that `.result__tag--ok` and `--under` make for
     the text. The state is also written in words, so removing every pixel of
     hue leaves the finding intact. WCAG 1.4.1. */
  .result :global(.result__tag) {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    border-color: currentColor;
  }
  .result :global(.result__tag--ok) { color: var(--c-verified); }
  /* --c-amber, not the hex it was hand-typed as. The token holds this exact
     value and carries the meaning ("at risk / below a threshold, and never
     colour alone") that this tag is the site's clearest example of; the literal
     said the same thing while opting out of the system that guarantees it. */
  .result :global(.result__tag--under) { color: var(--c-at-risk); }

  .result :global(.result__verdict) {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* TWO CLASSES THE SCRIPT WROTE THAT NOTHING STYLED, found 2026-08-04 by
     screenshotting the card rather than reading it. `result__kicker--tight` and
     `result__mission` are both applied in the submit handler and neither had a
     rule anywhere, so the modifier did nothing and the mission line inherited a
     body treatment from outside the component.

     The visible cost was the "Scored mission" kicker sitting hard against the
     last line of the verdict: the base kicker rule gives it a margin BELOW and
     none above, so at 1440 there was no gap at all where every other block in
     this card starts one. --stack-2 is the gap the card already uses between
     blocks, and "tight" is the distinction from --sec-gap rather than from
     nothing. */
  .result :global(.result__kicker--tight) { margin-top: var(--stack-2); }

  .result :global(.result__mission) {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .basis, .more {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) auto 0;
    max-width: 72ch;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }
  .more { font-size: var(--t-body); }
  .more a { color: var(--c-interactive); }
  .more a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive); outline-offset: 3px; }


  @media (max-width: 519px) {
    .result :global(.result__grid) { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
</style>
